> win32api is installed? It was useful but not essential with rpy1.

It could (through a "require" in the call to "setup()").

In theory, this is the same with rpy2. The import will not be made if 
R_HOME is defined:

# --- from rpy/rinterface/__init__.py ---

if len(R_HOME) == 0:
     if sys.platform == 'win32':
         try:
             import win32api
             import win32con
             hkey = win32api.RegOpenKeyEx(win32con.HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE,
                                          "Software\\R-core\\R",
                                          0, win32con.KEY_QUERY_VALUE )
             R_HOME = win32api.RegQueryValueEx(hkey, "InstallPath")[0]
             win32api.RegCloseKey( hkey )
         except:
             raise RuntimeError(
                 "Unable to determine R version from the registery." +\
                 "Calling the command 'R RHOME' does not return 
anything.\n" +\
                     "This might be because R.exe is nowhere in your Path.")



I will likely prefer wrapping the import statement in a try/except 
provide a more self-explicit error message upon ImportError.



L.

